es ABC and ABD are congruent right triangles with hypotenuse AB. Their perpendiculars to AB have the same foot H and length CH=DH=(3*4)/5=12/5. Since CD^2=CH^2+DH^2, CH is perpendicular to DH. Together with DH perpendicular to AB, this makes DH a height above ABC. Thus V=(1/3)*6*(12/5)=24/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_112"],"id":"common-foot-and-perpendicular-height","label":"Find the perpendicular height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-foot-and-perpendicular-height":["cnm_h111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_h120"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_h120","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_112"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 7; r=√6+√2-1","reason":"Write R=r+1. Adjacent centers are 2 apart, so R sin15°=1. Using cos30°=1-2sin²15° gives R²=4/(2-√3)=8+4√3=(√6+√2)². Hence r=√6+√2-1 and a+b+c=6+2-1=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_122","cnm_171"],"id":"thirty-degree-projection","label":"Centers and a 30-degree right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"thirty-degree-projection":["cnm_h181","cnm_266","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_266","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_122","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 4","reason":"Normalize the original side length to 2. The possible nondegenerate side-length triples are (1,1,1), (1,1,sqrt(3)), (1,sqrt(3),2), and (2,2,2). Each occurs, giving four congruence classes.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_8_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_110","cnm_074"],"id":"method1","label":"Classify by the number of original vertices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"method1":["cnm_120","cnm_122","cnm_185","cnm_137","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_120","cnm_122","cnm_185","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_055","cnm_110","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1+sqrt(2)","reason":"The outer centers form a square of side 2r, so its center-to-vertex distance is r*sqrt(2). Thus r*sqrt(2)=r+1, giving r=1/(sqrt(2)-1)=1+sqrt(2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_121","cnm_058","cnm_042","cnm_171"],"id":"center-distances","label":"Join the circle centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-distances":["cnm_h181","cnm_122","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_122","cnm_174","cnm_250","cnm_121","cnm_058","cnm_042","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Coordinator checked explicit alternate solutions against displayed English and revised the prior conventional-reading disposition.","derivedAnswer":"C under a crossing-arcs convention; the radius CD/2 also produces a meeting point and valid bisector.","reason":"The displayed English says arcs meeting at P inside the angle and asks for intersection, without requiring a crossing or two circle intersections. Let theta be the nonstraight angle and OC=OD=R. The chord midpoint M is inside the angle, CM=DM=CD/2, and OM bisects the angle by congruent right triangles. Equal-radius circles centered at C,D are tangent at M when r=CD/2; their arcs meet there. Thus strictly greater than CD/2 is not necessary for the stated construction. The narrower Chinese technical distinction between crossing and tangency was not supplied to the learner. Preserve the intended C key but do not enforce exclusive grading.","recordedAnswer":"C","status":"confirmed_ambiguous"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["circle_intersection_means_crossing_not_tangency","The construction uses crossing arcs with two intersection points; a tangent contact at radius CD/2 is not the source technical meaning of intersecting circles.","confirmed_answer_ambiguity"],"questionId":"cnm_127_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p188","cnm_120"],"id":"radius-cases-and-midpoint","label":"Distinguish crossing from tangent meeting","methodDirectNodeIds":{"radius-cases-and-midpoint":["cnm_127","cnm_111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_058"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_127","cnm_111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_058","cnm_055","cnm_p188","cnm_120"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 568","reason":"Each side must be at most 15. The number of ordered positive tuples summing to 32 is C(31,3)-4C(16,3)=2255. Exactly 15 have the form (a,b,a,b), where a+b=16; one of these is (8,8,8,8). Thus the cyclic-shift orbits number (2255-15)/4+(15-1)/2+1=568. Reflections are not additionally identified.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_111","cnm_122","cnm_h237","cnm_h060"],"id":"ordered-sides-and-cyclic-shifts","label":"Count side lists and account for rotational repetition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"ordered-sides-and-cyclic-shifts":["math:quadrilateral-side-feasibility","cnm_255","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_h064","math:stars-and-bars","math:symmetry-counting"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadrilateral-side-feasibility","cnm_255","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_h064","math:stars-and-bars","math:symmetry-counting","cnm_111","cnm_122","cnm_h237","cnm_h060"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: π + 6√3","reason":"The congruent 120° arcs have radius 1 because 2πr/3 = 2π/3. The hexago